 6. 🏛️ Rogue Dems face push for punishment 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 House Minority Leader Hakeem Jeffries (D-N.Y.) leaves a press conference at the Capitol on Monday. Photo: J. Scott Applewhite/AP Two centrist House Democrats who voted to help Republicans get a procedural motion across the finish line now face possible punishment from party leaders who say they were blindsided by the move, Axios' Andrew Solender writes. 

 Why it matters: House Minority Leader Hakeem Jeffries (D-N.Y.) is taking an uncharacteristically aggressive public approach to the defections, to the delight of some of his members. Reps. Jared Golden (D-Maine) and Marie Gluesenkamp Perez (D-Wash.) voted with Republicans to pass a "rule" — a procedural measure that sets the terms for House debate on a package of bills.

 Go deeper . Rep. Chuck Edwards (R-N.C.) talks to reporters at the Capitol yesterday. Photo: Eric Lee/Bloomberg via Getty Images Axios' Kate Santaliz, who broke the story on Rep. Chuck Edwards' conduct toward female staffers, writes: 

 Rep. Chuck Edwards (R-N.C.) was censured by the House yesterday after the Ethics Committee found he failed "to adhere to the spirit of rules prohibiting sexual harassment of and unwanted advances to House staffers."

 The vote amounts to a remarkable bipartisan rebuke, with the GOP leadership team and the vast majority of Edwards' own party joining Democrats to impose one of the House's most serious punishments. The Ethics Committee's findings follow Axios reporting that Edwards singled out two young female staffers in their 20s for special attention that multiple sources described as inappropriate. 

 Edwards and Rep. Ralph Norman (R-S.C.) were the only GOP "no" votes on the censure resolution, which passed 413-2. Keep reading ... Get Axios Hill Leaders , our weeknight newsletter from Capitol Hill. 

 
 
